{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And your stranger who is within your gates</b> On the level of peshat, a \"stranger of the gate\" for the world at large is a resident alien (גֵּר תּוֹשָׁב) who came to sojourn in the gates of our city and accepted upon himself the seven commandments of the children of Noah. He is the one called \"a stranger who eats carrion\" (Avodah Zarah 64b), concerning whom Scripture said (Deuteronomy 14:21), \"You may give it to the stranger who is within your gates, that he may eat it.\" Therefore the commandment was not stated about him as \"the native and the stranger shall not do any work,\" but rather He commands us that no work be done for our needs — like the young and the animal — but he himself is not subject to this commandment, and he does work for himself on the Sabbath. And the verse that says (Exodus 23:12), \"And the son of your maidservant and the stranger shall be refreshed,\" refers to a righteous convert (גֵּר צֶדֶק) who became a Jew and entered under our Torah, whom He commanded concerning the Sabbath and likewise concerning all the rest of the commandments, as He said, \"One Torah and one judgment shall there be for you and for the stranger who sojourns among you\" (Numbers 15:16), \"and for the stranger and for the native of the land\" (Numbers 15:29-30 — cf. ibid. v. 14, 15:29).\n\nBut we found that our Rabbis (Mekhilta here) interpreted the opposite: they said that \"and your stranger who is within your gates\" on the level of peshat refers to a righteous convert, and he is obligated like us in the cessation of work; \"and the son of your maidservant and the stranger shall be refreshed\" (Exodus 23:12) comes to include the uncircumcised resident alien. Their intent is that the one first mentioned — the circumcised stranger — is obligated like us, and the second verse comes to include the uncircumcised one. Therefore he was juxtaposed to the animal, for it says (ibid.), \"that your ox and your donkey may rest, and the son of your maidservant and the stranger may be refreshed\" — He commanded us regarding the cessation of all of them equally: that they not work for us, but they may do as they wish for themselves. And likewise, the slave and the stranger mentioned in the Ten Commandments are equal, and obligated in every law of the Sabbath like us, as He said (Deuteronomy 5:14), \"that your male servant and your maidservant may rest like you.\""], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Numbers 15:16 and then 'ולגר ולאזרח הארץ'; the latter phrase appears in Numbers 15:29-30 context but the exact wording matches Numbers 15:14 and 15:29. The citation is rendered as Ramban gives it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:11:35.174994004Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}